WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.400
Graph neural networks power everything from
citation search to recommendation,

00:00:05.480 --> 00:00:10.955
but they are alarmingly easy to fool by injecting a
few malicious nodes into the graph.

00:00:11.035 --> 00:00:16.127
This paper, from the Hong Kong Polytechnic
University and published at ICML 2024,

00:00:16.207 --> 00:00:21.873
presents the first collective certified robustness
scheme against graph injection attacks.

00:00:21.953 --> 00:00:26.854
Instead of certifying each node in isolation, which
is hopelessly pessimistic,

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:32.281
the authors certify a whole set of target nodes at
once by formulating the worst-case

00:00:32.361 --> 00:00:34.579
attacker as an optimization problem,

00:00:34.659 --> 00:00:40.454
then relaxing that hard integer program into a
linear program that solves in about a minute.

00:00:40.534 --> 00:00:42.752
The payoff is dramatic: on Citeseer,

00:00:42.832 --> 00:00:47.030
the certified ratio jumps from zero percent to over
eighty percent.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:51.064
Graph neural networks are the workhorses of graph
learning,

00:00:51.144 --> 00:00:57.773
but they can be broken by a graph injection attack
that slips a handful of malicious nodes into the graph.

00:00:57.853 --> 00:01:01.001
To trust these models we want certified robustness,

00:01:01.081 --> 00:01:05.241
a mathematical guarantee that predictions stay
stable under attack.

00:01:05.321 --> 00:01:10.810
The trouble is that every existing certificate for
injection attacks works node by node,

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:13.089
certifying each target in isolation.

00:01:13.169 --> 00:01:15.937
That sample-wise view is far too pessimistic,

00:01:16.017 --> 00:01:21.190
and in practice it certifies almost nothing once
the attacker gets a modest budget.

00:01:21.570 --> 00:01:25.073
Here is the key insight.

00:01:25.153 --> 00:01:28.656
In the real world,

00:01:28.736 --> 00:01:32.239
an attacker cannot conjure a different graph for
every node they want to fool.

00:01:32.319 --> 00:01:35.822
They inject one perturbed graph,

00:01:35.902 --> 00:01:39.405
and that single graph has to disrupt the entire set
of target nodes at the same time.

00:01:39.485 --> 00:01:42.988
If we certify the whole target set jointly instead
of one node at a time,

00:01:43.068 --> 00:01:46.571
the guarantee should be much stronger.

00:01:46.651 --> 00:01:50.154
Prior collective methods existed for
edge-modification attacks,

00:01:50.234 --> 00:01:53.737
but they assume a fixed receptive field and simply
do not carry over to injection attacks,

00:01:53.817 --> 00:01:57.320
which expand the receptive field by adding new edges.

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:03.087
This paper delivers the first collective certified
robustness scheme for graph neural

00:02:03.167 --> 00:02:05.338
networks against injection attacks.

00:02:05.418 --> 00:02:09.712
The authors cast certification as a worst-case
optimization problem,

00:02:09.792 --> 00:02:13.186
a binary integer quadratic constrained linear program,

00:02:13.266 --> 00:02:19.167
and then introduce a customized linearization that
relaxes this hard program into an ordinary

00:02:19.247 --> 00:02:22.126
linear program that can be solved efficiently.

00:02:22.206 --> 00:02:26.886
The result is almost model-agnostic: it works for
any message-passing GNN,

00:02:26.966 --> 00:02:32.160
and it buys huge gains in certified performance at
very little computational cost.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.516
The method builds on randomized smoothing,
specifically node-aware bi-smoothing,

00:02:37.596 --> 00:02:42.067
which randomly deletes edges and nodes to blur the
attacker's influence.

00:02:42.147 --> 00:02:47.629
The crucial observation is locality: an injected
node can only harm a target if at least

00:02:47.709 --> 00:02:51.484
one message-passing path between them survives the
smoothing.

00:02:51.564 --> 00:02:55.845
The authors upper-bound the probability of that
message interference,

00:02:55.925 --> 00:02:58.121
turn it into a certifying condition,

00:02:58.201 --> 00:03:03.556
and then model a worst-case attacker who tries to
make as many target nodes non-robust

00:03:03.636 --> 00:03:07.032
as possible under a budget on injected nodes and edges.

00:03:07.112 --> 00:03:11.077
That worst-case problem is the binary integer
quadratic program.

00:03:11.157 --> 00:03:14.680
Because it is NP-hard, they relax it to a linear program,

00:03:14.760 --> 00:03:16.449
and they offer two versions.

00:03:16.529 --> 00:03:19.673
The standard relaxation needs many extra variables,

00:03:19.753 --> 00:03:23.212
while their customized reformulation, Collective-LP-two,

00:03:23.292 --> 00:03:26.625
first collapses a quadratic term into a single vector,

00:03:26.705 --> 00:03:33.009
cutting the extra variables from order rho-squared
to order rho and improving both quality and speed.

00:03:33.089 --> 00:03:37.749
Since the linear program has a larger feasible
region than the integer one,

00:03:37.829 --> 00:03:42.110
its answer is always a sound lower bound on the
true certified ratio.

00:03:42.490 --> 00:03:46.928
The evaluation uses two standard citation graphs,
Cora-ML and Citeseer,

00:03:47.008 --> 00:03:51.065
with a few thousand nodes each, and two
representative backbones,

00:03:51.145 --> 00:03:54.755
a graph convolution network and a graph attention network.

00:03:54.835 --> 00:04:00.101
The attacker's budget is swept from twenty up to a
hundred and sixty injected nodes,

00:04:00.181 --> 00:04:04.174
with the per-node edge limit set to each graph's
average degree.

00:04:04.254 --> 00:04:11.492
The smoothed classifier is estimated with a hundred
thousand Monte Carlo samples at a one percent confidence level,

00:04:11.572 --> 00:04:16.010
and every linear program is solved with the MOSEK
solver through CVXPY.

00:04:16.390 --> 00:04:19.536
The headline result is a night-and-day improvement.

00:04:19.616 --> 00:04:21.940
As the number of injected nodes grows,

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.672
the sample-wise baseline collapses to zero
certified nodes,

00:04:25.752 --> 00:04:28.581
while the collective certificates hold strong.

00:04:28.661 --> 00:04:31.871
On Citeseer with a hundred and forty injected nodes,

00:04:31.951 --> 00:04:37.247
the standard collective relaxation certifies
seventy-three percent of targets and the

00:04:37.327 --> 00:04:40.790
customized version reaches eighty-one point two percent,

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.446
both against zero percent for sample-wise.

00:04:43.526 --> 00:04:48.507
The customized Collective-LP-two consistently
matches or beats the standard one,

00:04:48.587 --> 00:04:53.504
in one setting by over two hundred percent
relative, and it does so far faster,

00:04:53.584 --> 00:04:56.730
solving even the largest budgets in about a minute.

00:04:57.110 --> 00:05:01.033
How much does the linear relaxation cost us?

00:05:01.113 --> 00:05:05.036
To find out,

00:05:05.116 --> 00:05:09.039
the authors compare the relaxed program against the
exact integer program,

00:05:09.119 --> 00:05:13.042
which is only tractable for very small budgets.

00:05:13.122 --> 00:05:17.045
The gap is small: the customized relaxation loses
only about five percent in certified ratio,

00:05:17.125 --> 00:05:21.048
which also explains why the collective approach can
trail sample-wise at very tiny budgets.

00:05:21.128 --> 00:05:25.051
On runtime,

00:05:25.131 --> 00:05:29.054
the standard relaxation blows up past a thousand
seconds as the attack grows,

00:05:29.134 --> 00:05:33.057
while the customized version stays near a single minute,

00:05:33.137 --> 00:05:37.060
and at equal clean accuracy it dominates the
standard version on the accuracy-versus-certification trade-off.

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.901
To put numbers on it: on Citeseer at a hundred and
forty injected nodes,

00:05:41.981 --> 00:05:47.515
the certified ratio jumps from zero percent under
sample-wise certification to eighty-one

00:05:47.595 --> 00:05:51.047
point two percent with the customized collective method.

00:05:51.127 --> 00:05:54.831
That collective linear program solves in roughly
one minute.

00:05:54.911 --> 00:06:01.454
The customized relaxation improves on the standard
one by up to two hundred and sixteen percent relative,

00:06:01.534 --> 00:06:06.752
and it sits only about five percent below the exact
but far slower integer solution.

00:06:06.832 --> 00:06:09.653
In short, injecting five percent of the graph,

00:06:09.733 --> 00:06:14.510
the certified ratio climbs from zero to over eighty
percent on both datasets.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:17.015
The lesson is simple but powerful.

00:06:17.095 --> 00:06:21.359
Certifying a whole set of nodes together, instead
of one at a time,

00:06:21.439 --> 00:06:27.000
transforms a near-useless zero percent guarantee
into an eighty percent certified ratio

00:06:27.080 --> 00:06:29.075
against graph injection attacks.

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:32.447
A customized linear relaxation keeps this tractable,

00:06:32.527 --> 00:06:35.624
solving in about a minute even for large attacks.

00:06:35.704 --> 00:06:39.904
It is a concrete step toward provable defenses that
are practical,

00:06:39.984 --> 00:06:42.886
and because it shares the same smoothed model,

00:06:42.966 --> 00:06:49.630
it plugs right in alongside existing sample-wise
certificates to stay strong across every attack budget.
